[
  {
    "at": "2026-07-31T17:00:00Z",
    "note": "Kings stickman - 18:00 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/64ee6ddd-750f-4067-adfb-3e7e746d56ac.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/9f19cd8d-0d7b-4b78-8d2c-5aa6aefba4f7.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "In 1187 the King of England and the King of France shared a bed. On purpose. In public. Then had a monk write it down.\n\nRichard the Lionheart and Philip of France ate off the same plate every day, and at night their beds did not separate them.\n\nThis wasn't a scandal — it was a press release. Kings did this constantly, and the entire point was that everyone found out. It meant one thing: we are allies, I trust you, and nobody is getting stabbed tonight.\n\nA treaty you could nap in.\n\nHistorians have argued about it since 1948. Richard never explained himself. He didn't have to.\n\nWould you sign a treaty like that?\n\n#history #medievalhistory #richardthelionheart #historyfacts #historytok",
    "posted": "2026-07-31T17:04:36.411Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-07-31T17:30:00Z",
    "note": "Kings pastel - 18:30 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/1fb7ea42-e805-4775-8f95-1c6991d0303d.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/405bcbd5-ef97-438c-ad00-f3a007318095.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "Two kings. One bed. And a monk hired to write it down.\n\nIn 1187 Richard the Lionheart and Philip of France ate from the same plate every day, and at night their beds did not separate them. Everyone knew — because that was the entire point.\n\nThis wasn't gossip, it was diplomacy. A public signal that read: we are allies, I trust you, and nobody is getting stabbed tonight.\n\nA treaty you could nap in.\n\nHistorians have been arguing over what it meant since 1948. Richard never explained. He didn't need to.\n\nWould you sign a treaty like that?\n\n#history #medieval #kings #ancienthistory #didyouknow",
    "posted": "2026-07-31T17:35:09.821Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-07-31T18:00:00Z",
    "note": "Phryne watercolor v2 - 19:00 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/2780c0ae-5240-452f-9734-9e79acf6576a.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/d8336b0c-fed9-40b6-a638-e07cbcffed7f.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "She was on trial for her life in Ancient Athens — and her defense left the entire courtroom speechless.\n\nPhryne was the most famous courtesan in Greece. The priests accused her of blasphemy, and the penalty was death. When her lawyer Hypereides realized his words weren't landing, he did the unthinkable — he tore her robe open and argued that only the gods could carve a body that perfect.\n\nThe judges froze. They set her free.\n\nTwo thousand years later, historians still argue about whether it really happened.\n\nWould you have dared it?\n\n#history #ancientgreece #phryne #ancienthistory #historytok",
    "posted": "2026-07-31T18:04:36.633Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-07-31T19:00:00Z",
    "note": "Phryne fairy tale v2 - 20:00 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/5c362823-9939-4316-aa51-556c2c9134da.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/6eb4fc6d-4b58-4527-8f4b-d4c772876eaa.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "One gesture. That's all it took to overturn a death sentence in Ancient Athens.\n\nPhryne was the most famous courtesan in Greece — beautiful enough that sculptors used her as the model for Aphrodite herself. Then the priests accused her of blasphemy, a crime that carried the death penalty.\n\nHer lawyer begged the jury. Their faces didn't move. So he tore her robe open and told the court that only the gods could carve a body that perfect — and killing her would be an insult to them.\n\nThe judges froze. She walked free.\n\nHistorians still can't agree whether it actually happened.\n\nWould you have dared it?\n\n#history #greekhistory #phryne #historyfacts #athens",
    "posted": "2026-07-31T19:04:42.591Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-08-01T17:00:00Z",
    "note": "SATURDAY - Rasputin stickman - 18:00 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/22b6eb80-13ec-4ba2-820e-95818053ef65.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/2070cdf5-9ef7-4fa7-803e-e62a0934958a.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "The secret police watched his apartment and counted the women going inside. Dozens. Every single day. Duchesses and prostitutes in the same queue.\n\nGrigori Rasputin, a Siberian peasant, taught noblewomen that salvation came through sin — the more you sinned, the holier you got. Some kept his dirty underwear as holy relics.\n\nThen he reached the palace. The heir to Russia was a haemophiliac, and this filthy peasant mystic was the only man who could stop the bleeding. The Tsarina worshipped him.\n\nSo they decided to kill him. Poisoned cakes. Poisoned wine. He kept eating. They shot him — he got back up. They shot him again and dumped him in the river.\n\nExcept none of that happened. The autopsy found no poison, no water in his lungs, and one shot to the forehead. His killer made it up to sell a book.\n\n#history #rasputin #russianhistory #historyfacts #historytok",
    "posted": "2026-08-01T18:25:27.492Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-08-01T17:30:00Z",
    "note": "SATURDAY - Rasputin pastel - 18:30 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/0505cb47-ae89-43e8-8f78-4726a488812c.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/ede29131-ecb8-4c74-b019-3070f1847aaa.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "Dozens of women a day. Duchesses queuing alongside prostitutes. The secret police sat outside and counted them.\n\nGrigori Rasputin was a Siberian peasant who convinced Russian noblewomen that salvation came through sin — and the more you sinned, the holier you became. Some kept his dirty underwear as relics.\n\nIt got him into the palace. The heir to the throne was a haemophiliac, and this filthy mystic was the only person alive who could stop the bleeding. The Tsarina worshipped him for it.\n\nSo they killed him. Poisoned cakes, poisoned wine — he ate through all of it. Shot once, he stood back up. Shot again, dumped in the river.\n\nNone of it is true. The autopsy found no poison and no water in his lungs, just a single bullet to the forehead. His murderer invented the rest to sell a book.\n\n#history #rasputin #russia #historyuncovered #didyouknow",
    "posted": "2026-08-01T18:27:15.498Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-08-02T17:00:00Z",
    "note": "SUNDAY - Toys stickman - 18:00 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/6a8c0d02-a27f-4cd2-a3a8-60b803b0c097.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/7fcf21c4-a35f-4f3f-97a9-f22faa74fee0.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "Three things everyone repeats about the history of this device. All three are false.\n\nCleopatra and the gourd full of bees? No ancient source says it. The story was invented in 1992.\n\nArchaeologists really did rebuild a 29,000-year-old stone object from 14 fragments. Eight inches long. It was also used as a hammer.\n\nAnd the Victorian doctor everyone credits with curing hysteria? He refused to treat women with it, and said so in writing. That story traces back to a single book published in 1999. In 2018 historians checked every source it cited. Not one of them said it.\n\nBoth of the myths you believe were invented in your lifetime.\n\n#history #historyfacts #victorian #mythbusting #didyouknow",
    "posted": "2026-08-02T19:50:00.424Z"
  },
  {
    "at": "2026-08-02T17:30:00Z",
    "note": "SUNDAY - Toys paper - 18:30 BST",
    "url": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/5d633655-ffaf-4d58-91d5-7fa5bc6511ec.mp4",
    "cover": "https://d2ol7oe51mr4n9.cloudfront.net/user_3E5kpoO7CXWYDEVasBRvd0P7wSt/2a89455e-d3ee-4fe9-b2a3-73fa6ec190f4.jpg",
    "trial": "manual",
    "caption": "The most repeated fact on this subject has no ancient source at all. It was made up in 1992.\n\nWhat is real: archaeologists reconstructed a 29,000-year-old stone object from 14 separate fragments. Eight inches long. It had also been used as a hammer.\n\nIn 1869 a doctor built a padded table powered by a steam engine so large it lived in the next room and came through the wall. He called it the Manipulator.\n\nAnd the man everyone credits with curing hysteria in 1880? He refused to use it on women and wrote that down. The whole story comes from one book published in 1999. Historians checked its sources in 2018. Not one supported it.\n\n#history #historyfacts #victorianera #mythbusting #historytok",
    "posted": "2026-08-02T20:20:28.504Z"
  }
]
